# .env.example - Production-Grade IaC with Ansible
#
# Copy this file to .env and pick your own username/password for each
# environment - there is no built-in default. .env is git-ignored (see
# .gitignore) and must never be committed.
#
# This is not just a convenience file. mock_device_server.py reads this
# exact same .env file (via its own _load_dotenv(), ported from
# CI-CD_Pipeline_with_Git's iac_lib.py) to decide what credentials to
# expect on every incoming request. Whatever you put here is genuinely
# what the mock fleet checks against - not a value a playbook sends that
# the mock quietly ignores. If .env doesn't exist, or a value is missing,
# requests to that environment's devices fail with a real 401.
#
# Playbooks read these same values back with lookup('env', 'RESTCONF_...'),
# never by hardcoding a username/password in a playbook, role, or
# group_vars file (see group_vars/prod.yml and group_vars/staging.yml for
# why credentials are deliberately absent from both).
#
# In a real GitLab pipeline (lab 12), these same four values are supplied
# instead as Protected CI/CD variables (Settings > CI/CD > Variables) -
# GitLab injects them directly into the job's environment, no .env file
# involved there. Use the same values in both places if you want a local
# run and a GitLab run to behave identically.
#
# Naming convention: RESTCONF_<ENVIRONMENT NAME UPPERCASED>_USERNAME / _PASSWORD.
# See mock_device_server.py's restconf_credential_env_vars() for where
# this is enforced.

RESTCONF_PROD_USERNAME=pick-any-username
RESTCONF_PROD_PASSWORD=pick-any-password

RESTCONF_STAGING_USERNAME=pick-any-username
RESTCONF_STAGING_PASSWORD=pick-any-password
